2FPalace_of_Nestor)\n\n\n\nFact-checked by Grok 5 months ago\n\n# Palace of 
Nestor\n\nThe **Palace of Nestor** is a major Mycenaean palace complex located 
on Englianos hill near the modern town of [Pylos](/page/Pylos) in 
[Messenia](/page/Messenia), southwestern [Greece](/page/Greece), dating to the 
Late [Bronze Age](/page/Bronze_Age) and serving as the administrative center of 
the kingdom of [Pylos](/page/Pylos).[[[1]](#ref-1)](#ref-1) Constructed in the 
early [13th century](/page/13th_century) BCE during the Late Helladic III 
period, the site features a large, multi-building layout with over 90 rooms, 
including a central *[megaron](/page/Megaron)* (throne room), storage 
facilities, workshops, baths, and an advanced drainage system, reflecting Minoan
architectural influences such as [ashlar](/page/Ashlar) masonry and light 
wells.[[[2]](#ref-2)](#ref-2) The palace was destroyed by fire around 1200 BCE, 
likely during a period of widespread Mycenaean collapse, and was never 
reoccupied, preserving its structures and artifacts in remarkable 
condition.[[[3]](#ref-3)](#ref-3) Excavations at the site began in 1939 under 
Greek archaeologist Konstantinos Kourouniotes and American archaeologist Carl W.
Blegen, but were interrupted by [World War II](/page/World_War_II); systematic 
work resumed from 1952 to 1966, led by Blegen in collaboration with [Spyridon 
Marinatos](/page/Spyridon_Marinatos), under the auspices of the [University of 
Cincinnati](/page/University_of_Cincinnati) and the American School of Classical
Studies at Athens.[[[3]](#ref-3)](#ref-3) Blegen's team uncovered the palace's 
extensive layout, including a two-story main building and surrounding 
structures, along with vibrant frescoes depicting scenes of 
[nature](/page/Nature), processions, and daily life, such as an octopus motif 
and a \"Tarzan\" griffin-slaying figure.[[[4]](#ref-4)](#ref-4) The site's 
preservation allowed for detailed stratigraphic analysis, revealing it as one of
the best-excavated and most intact examples of Mycenaean palatial 
architecture.[[[4]](#ref-4)](#ref-4) The palace is named for the legendary king 
Nestor, a wise elder in Homer's *[Iliad](/page/Iliad)* and 
*[Odyssey](/page/Odyssey)*, whose \"sandy [Pylos](/page/Pylos)\" aligns with the
site's coastal location, bridging myth and history.[[[2]](#ref-2)](#ref-2) Its 
significance lies in the discovery of over 1,100 [Linear B](/page/Linear_B) clay
tablets in the archives room, baked hard by the destruction fire, which record 
administrative details like [land tenure](/page/Land_tenure), taxation, 
religious offerings, and [trade](/page/Trade) in goods such as oil, wine, and 
[bronze](/page/Bronze), providing the earliest evidence of [Greek 
language](/page/Greek_language) use and insights into Mycenaean bureaucratic 
organization.[[[3]](#ref-3)](#ref-3) Nearby tholos tombs and ongoing regional 
surveys further illuminate the palace's role in a hierarchical society with 
warrior elites and extensive maritime networks.[[[1]](#ref-1)](#ref-1) Today, 
the site is a protected archaeological [park](/page/Park), offering a window 
into the complexities of pre-classical Greek 
civilization.[[[3]](#ref-3)](#ref-3)\n\n## Location and Setting\n\n### 
Geographical Position\n\nThe Palace of Nestor is situated at Epano Englianos 
(also known as Ano Englianos), a hilltop site in the [Messenia](/page/Messenia) 
region of southwestern [Peloponnese](/page/Peloponnese), [Greece](/page/Greece),
with precise coordinates of 37°01′38″N 21°41′42″E.[[[1]](#ref-1)](#ref-1) This 
location places it approximately 17 kilometers north of the modern town of 
[Pylos](/page/Pylos) by road, aligning with the ancient harbor area associated 
with the Homeric kingdom.[[[5]](#ref-5)](#ref-5) The site has been identified as
the likely center of the Mycenaean …(truncated)"
