of Pylos. Bronze Age Treasures of Messenia”, in order to fulfill the need of 
exhibiting to the public in Greece and abroad some of the approximately 1,400 
unique and precious grave goods that accompanied the burial of a 30-35-year-old 
man, in a pit grave dated back to 1450 BC. The discovery of the grave has been 
followed with great interest in Greece and internationally due to its intact 
preservation state and the significance of the findings which came to 
light.\n\nThe grave is globally known as “The Griffin Warrior Tomb”, a name 
which was given to it because of a very rare ivory plaque found by the feet of 
the deceased representing a griffin in a rocky landscape. The grave is located 
near the Chamber Tomb IV, c. 150 metres NE of the Palace of Nestor and was 
excavated in 2015.\n\nThe exhibition “Princes of Pylos” is the capstone of a 
long-lasting and fruitful collaboration between the Ephorate of Antiquities of 
Messenia and the archaeologists Professor Jack L. Davis and Dr. Sharon Stocker 
