      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "This digitized record refers to Arthur J. Evans’s 
1925 monograph \"The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world and a
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,\" 
which discusses the so‑called Ring of Nestor and compares it to a broader 
assemblage of Minoan and Mycenaean gold signet rings and bead seals. The work 
situates the ring within funerary and religious contexts of the Aegean Bronze 
Age and is a key early publication defining the object as a Minoan‑style gold 
signet ring connected with Nestor and a Boeotian findspot at Thisbe.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "This 2013 article by Theodore Eliopoulos analyzes the
iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates. It revisits specific iconographic features of the ring, 
such as motifs comparable to incurved altars, to assess whether the imagery 
supports or undermines the ring’s attribution as a genuine Minoan or Mycenaean 
artifact. The paper argues that certain iconographic correspondences, including 
the relation between the ring’s depiction and incurved altars, tend to support 
authenticity, while also acknowledging that aspects of the iconography could 
reflect early‑20th‑century cultural practices. It emphasizes the long‑standing 
scholarly controversy over the genuine nature of both the Ring of Nestor and the
Ring of Minos, and stresses the importance of iconographic analysis when 
technical tests yield contradictory results.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "This research work on \"The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring
