    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "This 2019 paper (Hudler, \"No (e)scape?\") discusses 
problematic or doubtful gem impressions (gemmae dubitandae), using the object 
known as the \"Ring of Nestor\" as an example. It notes that such objects become
problematic when their archaeological properties are fully considered, and 
situates the ring within debates over authenticity and the evidentiary value of 
unprovenanced or poorly documented artifacts.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "This Metropolitan Museum perspectives article on 
historic images of the Greek Bronze Age summarizes Nanno Marinatos's arguments 
that two famous gold rings, the Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor, are modern
forgeries produced by the restorer and copyist Emile Gilliéron fils. It explains
that these rings played an influential role in shaping early reconstructions of 
Minoan religion and art, and highlights how questions about their authenticity 
affect interpretations of Minoan iconography and the history of archaeological 
restoration at Knossos.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "This Journal of Hellenic Studies article, \"The Ring 
of Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and A Sepulchral Treasure of 
Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,\" analyzes the 
iconography of the Ring of Nestor and associated gold signet rings and bead 
seals from a burial at Thisbe in Boeotia. It interprets the ring's scenes as 
representing aspects of the Minoan afterlife and sacred landscape, and discusses
the ring's artistic and religious significance within the broader corpus of 
Minoan and Mycenaean glyptic.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
