Ashmolean Museum."}, {"id": "20260628000950923068_b91158ae", "type": "Source", 
"source_type": "paper", "title": "The Lord of the Gold Rings", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/116392582/The_Lord_of_the_Gold_Rings"}, {"id": 
"20260628000951046687_2074aac2", "type": "Document", "source_id": 
"20260628000950865390_33d97640", "text": "This 2013 article by Theodore 
Eliopoulos analyzes the iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of 
Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates. It revisits specific iconographic 
features of the ring, such as motifs comparable to incurved altars, to assess 
whether the imagery supports or undermines the ring’s attribution as a genuine 
Minoan or Mycenaean artifact. The paper argues that certain iconographic 
correspondences, including the relation between the ring’s depiction and 
incurved altars, tend to support authenticity, while also acknowledging that 
aspects of the iconography could reflect early‑20th‑century cultural practices. 
It emphasizes the long‑standing scholarly controversy over the genuine nature of
both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, and stresses the importance of 
iconographic analysis when technical tests yield contradictory results."}, 
