[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Synthesis for article drafting: The
Ring of Nestor can be treated as a Late Bronze Age gold signet ring in the 
Ashmolean Museum, widely labeled as Late Minoan I and often associated with 
Crete, depicting four ritual or cult scenes on its bezel. Evans (1925) 
introduced it as part of a Boeotian treasure from Thisbe and connected it with 
Minoan afterlife beliefs. Later scholarship (including Eliopoulos’s iconographic
study and work on the pseudo‑Minoan Nestor ring and its Egyptian iconography) 
uses the ring as a case study in debates about Minoan/Mycenaean religious 
imagery and the possibility of early 20th‑century forgeries. At the same time, 
an Ashmolean collections entry, an Ashmolean image-library listing, and 
independent scholarly references confirm the object as a gold signet ring in 
that museum, dated broadly to ca. 1600–1450 BCE / Late Minoan I. The article 
should present the ring both as an archaeological/museum object and as a 
contested artifact whose date, provenance (Crete vs. Thisbe), and authenticity 
have been discussed for decades, clearly distinguishing between institutional 
cataloguing data and interpretive or critical scholarship.', 'query_id': 
'20260628000909471425_72ce89f6'})
