Lead section\n- Begin with a concise lead that defines the Ring of Nestor, 
establishes its general date and cultural context, notes that it is a gold 
signet ring now in the Ashmolean Museum, and briefly indicates that its 
provenance, date, and authenticity have been the subject of scholarly debate.\n-
The lead should stand alone as a summary and reflect the relative importance of 
topics (object description, archaeological/historical context, interpretations, 
authenticity debates, and current status).\n\n2. Article scope\n- Treat the Ring
of Nestor primarily as the Late Bronze Age gold signet ring in the Ashmolean 
Museum, commonly described as Late Minoan I and associated with Crete, while 
explaining Evans\x019s original association with a sepulchral treasure from 
Thisbe in Boeotia.\n- If the name "Ring of Nestor" is used for other objects or 
in other contexts in the evidence, mention these briefly in a dedicated "Name 
and terminology" or similar section, clarifying that the article focuses on the 
Ashmolean ring.\n\n3. Structure and coverage\nOrganize the body into clear 
sections with informative headings, for example (you may adjust titles to fit 
the evidence):\n- Name and description\n- Discovery and provenance\n- Historical
and archaeological context\n- Iconography and scholarly interpretations\n- 
Authenticity and forgery debates\n- Museum history and modern 
reception\n\nWithin these sections:\n- Summarize the Ashmolean catalogue 
information (gold material, Late Minoan I / ca. 1600\t1450 BCE dating, signet 
function, association with Crete and with the Ashmolean collection), citing the 
collections entry and any secondary scholarly references that repeat this 
information.\n- Describe the ring in general physical terms as far as the 
evidence allows (signet ring, gold, Late Minoan I period, four ritual/cult 
scenes on the bezel). Do NOT invent dimensions or details not supported in the 
evidence; if weight or specific details are only attested in user-generated 
sources (e.g., Pinterest, Facebook), either omit them or attribute and qualify 
them appropriately as non-scholarly.\n- Explain Evans\x019s 1925 treatment of 
the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and 
bead seals from Thisbe in Boeotia and his framing of the ring as providing a 
"glimpse into the Minoan after-world" and Minoan religious or funerary beliefs, 
citing his monograph.\n- Summarize later scholarly work: the iconographic study 
"Some Observations on the Iconography of the \x1aRing of Nestor\x1a" 
(Eliopoulos) and the paper "The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian 
Iconography" (and any other relevant modern studies in the graph). Report their 
main themes: use of the ring as a case study in Aegean forgeries, focus on 
ritual imagery and incurved altars, possible Egyptian parallels, and arguments 
for and against the ring\x019s authenticity.\n- Use evidence from the Hesperia 
article "The Lord of the Gold Rings" and related summaries to situate the ring 
in broader Late Bronze Age contexts (e.g., comparison with the Griffin Warrior 
grave rings, Minoan-manufactured signet rings with ritual scenes, links between 
Pylos and Crete).\n- Clearly distinguish between institutional cataloguing data 
(Ashmolean dating and description) and scholarly interpretations or doubts 
(Evans\x019s Minoan afterworld reading, later authenticity debates, 
pseudo-Minoan/Egyptian iconography analysis, suggestions of 1920s cultural 
influences).\n\n4. Neutrality and words to watch\n- Maintain a neutral, 
descriptive tone. Do not state that the ring is authentic or a forgery in 
Wikipedia\x019s voice; instead, describe the positions of named scholars or 
groups of scholars and indicate that authenticity has been debated for 
generations.\n- Avoid peacock terms, promotional adjectives, contentious labels,
editorializing adverbs, and vague attributions. Attribute views precisely (e.g.,
"Evans interpreted\x1a", "a 2013 article argues\x1a", "later authors have 
treated\x1a").\n\n5. Citations and references\n- Provide bracketed numerical 
citations [1], [2], etc. for all substantive factual claims.\n- Use each unique 
source URL as a separate reference entry. Where multiple claims draw from the 
same source, reuse the same bracketed number.\n- At the end of the article, add 
a "References" section listing each numbered reference with a full URL (and, 
where available in the graph, a short indication of title or author). Do NOT 
include any Wikipedia URLs or the prohibited page.\n\n6. Handling weak 
sources\n- If you use information that comes only from user-generated or 
social-media-type content (e.g., Pinterest, Facebook), clearly attribute it 
