Later scholarship has reassessed this iconographic interpretation within broader
studies of Minoan sacred landscapes and ritual imagery. Analyses of Minoan gold 
rings highlight features such as sacred sites rendered in miniature, portable 
table altars or incurved altars, and seats for animals like griffins. The Ring 
of Nestor has been cited as an example in which a griffin’s seat and altar forms
comparable to those documented in Minoan art are combined, and these elements 
have been used to argue that its imagery reflects Minoan cult practice and the 
use of specific altar types in ritual performances.[9][2]

A modern article titled “Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of 
Nestor’” focuses specifically on the ring’s imagery in the context of Aegean 
forgeries and authenticity debates.[2] The article is described as an analysis 
of the iconography surrounding the Ring of Nestor that aims to contribute to the
ongoing discourse regarding the ring’s significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art,
drawing on historical scholarship and recent discoveries to question and affirm 
previous conclusions related to the ring’s motifs and their implications.[2] 
Among the key points summarized in the article’s abstract and AI-generated key 
takeaways are statements that the connection between incurved altars and the 
ring’s depiction supports its authenticity and that generations of scholars have
debated the genuineness of both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos.[2]

The study “The Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography” examines 
how Minoan and Mycenaean gold rings were manufactured and proposes a typology 
for such rings, using the so‑called Nestor Ring as a notable example.[20] It 
characterizes the ring as having Egyptian iconography and discusses its imagery 
in relation to Egyptian motifs, thereby contributing to debates about its 
cultural attribution and potential hybrid or non-Aegean iconographic 
influences.[20] The paper’s description emphasizes that observations draw upon 
historical scholarship and recent discoveries to question and affirm previous 
conclusions related to the ring’s motifs and their implications for Minoan and 
Mycenaean art.[20]

The iconographic article noted above also observes that some aspects of the Ring
of Nestor’s imagery may reflect cultural practices of the 1920s, suggesting that
if the ring were a modern creation its designer might have incorporated 
contemporary motifs or arrangements.[2] This possibility is presented alongside 
arguments that specific cult features, such as incurved altars, align closely 
with genuine Minoan iconography. As a result, scholars have used both 
similarities and anomalies in the imagery to argue for or against authenticity, 
and interpretations of the ring’s scenes are closely tied to positions on its 
genuineness.[2][20][22]

## Authenticity and forgery debates

Since its introduction by Evans, the Ring of Nestor has been at the centre of 
prolonged debates over authenticity.[2][7][8][20][22] The *Journal of Ancient 
Egyptian Interconnections* article on the “pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring” reports 
that the ring was found in 1924 and notes that Evans accepted it as authentic 
while many other scholars condemned it as a forgery.[20] This summary 
illustrates the extent of disagreement over the ring’s status and frames it as 
an object whose authenticity has been contested for decades.

Hudler’s discussion of “gemmae dubitandae” — doubtful gems — in Aegean 
archaeology includes the Ring of Nestor as an example of an object whose 
archaeological properties and authenticity are problematic.[7] By placing it in 
this category, Hudler emphasizes that uncertainties about provenance and context
make it difficult to treat the ring as secure evidence without acknowledging its
contested status.[7]

Nanno Marinatos has argued that the Ring of Nestor, together with the Ring of 
Minos, is a modern forgery created by the restorer and copyist Emile Gilliéron 
fils, and these rings play a central role in her critique of Evans’s 
reconstructions at Knossos.[8][10] A Metropolitan Museum of Art essay on 
historic images of the Greek Bronze Age and a study of Evans’s creation of the 
“vision of Knossos” both discuss Marinatos’s arguments and highlight the role of
the Ring of Nestor in debates about the reliability of some widely disseminated 
images of Minoan religion and art.[8][10] In this view, the ring is considered 
part of a problematic legacy in which restored or fabricated objects influenced 
modern conceptions of the Greek Bronze Age.

In contrast, other studies have presented technical and iconographic 
observations that support the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor. A paper 
disseminated via ResearchGate titled “The ‘Ring of Nestor’” reports new 
technical observations that serve to support the ring’s authenticity and notes 
that it was once again placed on display in the Ashmolean Museum in 1995.[22] 
The iconographic study “Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of 
Nestor’” similarly argues that the depiction of incurved altars and other cult 
features is consistent with authentic Minoan iconography and thus weighs in 
favour of genuineness.[2] By emphasizing iconographic continuities with securely
provenanced Minoan gold rings, these authors present the ring as likely 
authentic despite earlier suspicions.[2][22]

Both sides of the debate acknowledge that the Ring of Nestor has had a 
significant impact on interpretations of Minoan religion and on the visual 
language used to represent the Bronze Age Aegean. The disagreement concerns 
whether the ring’s imagery should be treated as archaeological evidence or as a 
modern construction that nevertheless shaped scholarly and public 
imaginaries.[2][7][8][10][20][22]

## Museum history and modern reception

Multiple sources report that the Ring of Nestor is housed in the Ashmolean 
Museum, University of Oxford.[5][6][18][22] A Brandeis University thesis on 
Bronze Age iconography cites the Ashmolean collection record directly, giving 
the description “Signet ring; Ring of Nestor. ca. 1600–1450 B.C.E. Metal (Gold).
Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford” and providing the collections URL, which
corresponds to the object number 478083.[18] Search results in the Ashmolean 
Museum Image Library similarly list an item identified as a signet ring, with 
the preferred descriptive title “Signet ring” and the popular title “The Ring of
Nestor”, dated to the Late Minoan I period.[18]

The ResearchGate summary of the article “The ‘Ring of Nestor’” notes that, 
following technical re‑evaluation, the ring was once again placed on display in 
1995.[22] This indicates that the Ashmolean has adjusted its exhibition 
practices in response to scholarly debate over authenticity, and that the ring’s
museum history includes periods of display and possible withdrawal linked to the
evolving assessment of its status.[22]

The Ring of Nestor has also played a prominent role in modern discussions of the
historiography and visual culture of Aegean archaeology. A Metropolitan Museum 
of Art essay on historic images of the Greek Bronze Age highlights Marinatos’s 
critique of Evans’s use of objects such as the Ring of Nestor and underscores 
that such images have strongly influenced public and scholarly conceptions of 
Minoan religion and art, even when their authenticity has been questioned.[8] 
Similarly, analyses of Evans’s legacy and the creation of the “vision of 
Knossos” discuss the Ring of Nestor and related rings as central to debates over
facts and forgeries in the interpretation of Minoan Crete.[10]

Beyond scholarly publications, social‑media posts and online image boards 
circulate photographs and brief descriptions of the Ring of Nestor. A Pinterest 
entry presents the ring as a Late Minoan gold signet ring showing four cult 
scenes and attributes it to the Ashmolean Museum, while a Facebook group post 
describes it as an approximately 3,700‑year‑old gold signet ring from Crete 
housed at the Ashmolean.[5][6] These user-generated sources are not 
peer‑reviewed, but they indicate a broader public interest in the ring as an 
example of Bronze Age Aegean goldwork and as an emblematic object associated 
with Minoan religious imagery.

